e genuinely regenerated rather than smoothed.\n4. **Transition-path kinetics** \u2014 distribution of transition times between torsion basins; fraction of correct transitions vs. missed/fast-forwarded transitions.\n5. (Secondary) backbone RMSD/RMSF, TIC-2 overlap, Ramachandran coverage \u2014 standard MDGEN-style global checks, to confirm fine-dynamics claims aren't just global-structure matches.\n\n**Baselines / controls to establish that recovery is real:**\n- **Linear/interpolation baseline** between the two conditioning frames (the null model: what you'd get without a learned dynamics model). MDGEN must beat it on autocorrelation, spectra, and transition-time statistics, not just on distributions.\n- **\"Subsampled ground truth\"** (the coarse input itself) as a lower bound showing what subsampling destroys.\n- **Ground-truth full-rate MD** as the upper bound / reference.\n- **Negative control / permutation test:** shuffled or time-reversed generated frames should fail the autocorrelation/spectral tests \u2014 verifying the metrics are sensitive to genuine temporal ordering, not just marginal distributions.\n- **Frequency-of-success test:** compare MDGEN vs. interpolation across all test segments/proteins with paired statistics (e.g., paired bootstrap on KL divergence and spectral-distance metrics).\n\n**Decision criterion:** MDGEN \"recovers fast dynamics\" if, on held-out data, it (a) matches ground-truth torsion autocorrelations and PSDs within a pre-specified tolerance band, (b) significantly outperforms the interpolation baseline on those same metrics, and (c) does not merely match marginal torsion distributions while failing temporal statistics.\n\n## 3. Consequential assumptions and uncertainties\n\n- **Ground truth for fast dynamics:** the design assumes the *fully sampled* MD trajectories are a trustworthy reference (force-field realism aside), and that torsion-angle oscillations at the native timestep are statistically stationary enough that held-out segments represent them. If only subsampled data exist, there is no gold standard and the question becomes unanswerable as posed.\n- **No reference setup exists in the workspace:** no MDGEN code, hyperparameters, checkpoint, or dataset (ATLAS/MDsim) is supplied, so all implementation details (model size, \u03b4t, noise schedule, dataset choice) are assumptions from the published MDGEN setup, not repo-verified facts.\n- **Compute/feasibility:** original MDGEN training used substantial GPU resources; this environment has none. Feasibility of the design depends entirely on whether the researcher can provision a GPU environment and the reference datasets \u2014 flagged as an assumption, not a finding.\n- **Conditioning regime sensitivity:** results may hinge on \u0394t and the number of generated fine steps; the multi-\u0394t sweep is included to cover this, but the \"right\" operating point is uncertain.\n- **Metric-sensitivity risk:** spectral/autocorrelation agreement on dihedral angles could be achieved while missing slow transitions, or vice versa; the metric battery and negative controls mitigate but do not eliminate interpretation ambiguity.\n- **Statistical power:** number of test proteins/segments must be large enough for paired bootstrap comparisons to detect the MDGEN-vs-interpolation gap; exact power is uncertain without dataset access.\n- **Generalization scope:** claims are per-dataset (e.g., ATLAS-like proteins); extrapolating to new folds/systems is an additional, separate assumption.", "evidence": "Inspection findings (concrete): /workspace contains only task.json (464 bytes) stating the question and a track.md note \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" No MDGEN code, configs, checkpoints, trajectory data, torsion-analysis scripts, or documentation exist. Environment checks: python3 present but numpy and mdtraj import failures (ModuleNotFoundError), nvidia-smi fails (no GPU driver), network disabled, /tmp has ~100G tmpfs. Therefore the proposed approach draws on general knowledge of MDGEN (diffusion-based SE(3)-equivariant model trained on subsampled MD trajectories, conditioned on start/end frames of coarse segments, generating intermediate fine-timestep frames; evaluated originally with distributions, TICs, etc., on datasets like ATLAS/MDsim) and standard MD torsion-analysis practice; no repo-specific facts constrain the design. No experiment was run and none is claimed.", "stage": "plan", "summary": "Workspace inspected; MDGEN fast-dynamics recovery experiment approach proposed", "uncertainties": ["No ground-truth fine-timestep trajectories may be available; if only subsampled data exist the question as posed is unanswerable and the design would need a proxy reference (e.g., short re-run MD).", "No reference MDGEN setup details (code, hyperparameters, datasets, checkpoints) exist in the workspace; all implementation specifics are assumptions from the published method, subject to researcher clarification.", "Compute feasibility: original MDGEN training is GPU-intensive; this environment has no GPU, packages, or network, so execution feasibility depends entirely on resources the researcher provisions.", "Choice of subsampling interval \u0394t and number of generated fine steps strongly conditions results; the multi-\u0394t sweep covers a range but the intended operating point is unknown.", "Metric interpretation risk: agreement on torsion distributions vs. temporal statistics can diverge; the negative controls mitigate but exact decision thresholds require dataset statistics to calibrate."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-2085f04bb26f42189b83167ab632da8e", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Workspace inspected; MDGEN fast-dynamics recovery experiment approach proposed", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
