 description and general MD-analysis practice, and are labeled as such.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Rationale\n\n### 1.1 Precise hypothesis\n\n**H\u2081 (primary):** For held-out protein test segments, MDGEN \u2014 conditioned only on coarse-interval (start, end) frames sampled at interval \u0394t \u2014 generates intermediate fine-timestep frames whose torsion-angle *temporal statistics* (autocorrelation functions, power spectra, transition-time distributions) match those of the held-out fully sampled ground-truth MD significantly better than a structure-only interpolation baseline, at a pre-specified significance level and with a pre-specified minimum effect size.\n\n**H\u2080 (null):** MDGEN's fine-timestep torsion statistics are statistically indistinguishable from those of linear interpolation between the conditioning frames (i.e., the model adds no fast-dynamics information beyond endpoint structure).\n\nA secondary, clearly separated hypothesis (H\u2082, ablation): removing the end-frame conditioning (start-frame-only variant) degrades recovery of the *oscillation frequency structure* more than it degrades marginal torsion distributions \u2014 testing whether MDGEN learns genuine dynamics or endpoint-guided interpolation.\n\n### 1.2 Why subsampling destroys fast torsion dynamics\n\nMolecular dynamics at native timestep \u03b4t (typically 1\u201350 fs, saved every ~1\u201310 ps in common benchmarks) contains fast torsional oscillations: dihedral angles undergo bounded librations about metastable basins with characteristic periods far shorter than the subsampling interval \u0394t (e.g., 1 ns). Subsampling at \u0394t \u226b the libration period **aliases** these oscillations: (a) the time series is decimated below twice its highest frequency (Nyquist), so oscillatory content is irrecoverably lost or folded into spurious low-frequency components; (b) the apparent dynamics of the subsampled series is dominated by slow basin transitions, and autocorrelation functions of dihedral angles become corrupted or misleading; (c) intermediate configurations \u2014 which determine short-time autocorrelation decay, spectral peaks, and transition-path timing \u2014 are simply absent. Any quantity computed on the subsampled trajectory that depends on sub-\u0394t structure is therefore destroyed or aliased, which is precisely why it serves as a meaningful \"lower bound\" control.\n\n### 1.3 Why MDGEN could in principle recover fast dynamics \u2014 or plausibly fail\n\n*Grounded in the published MDGEN method (Schneuing et al., 2024, arXiv:2409.12174; labeled as published-method knowledge, not workspace-verified):* MDGEN is an SE(3)-equivariant diffusion generative model with a chromatic-style protein backbone, trained on MD trajectories subsampled at a coarse interval \u0394t (e.g., 1 ns on ATLAS and MDsim). Each training example is a short segment (start frame, end frame \u0394t apart, and the intermediate fine-timestep frames); the model is conditioned on the two endpoint frames and trained to generate the intermediate frames, autoregressively filling in fine steps within each coarse interval.\n\n**Plausible route to success:** the training objective, conditioned on endpoints, must place probability mass on physically plausible intermediate conformations. If the model learns the *conditional distribution* of intermediate states given endpoints \u2014 rather than merely a deterministic geodesic between them \u2014 the ensemble of generated intermediates can reproduce second-order (and higher) temporal statistics of the true fine dynamics: short-time autocorrelation decay, oscillation amplitudes, and spectral content, even though each individual generated trajectory is not a faithful sample-by-sample replay of the ground truth.\n\n**Plausible routes to failure (which the design must be able to detect):**\n1. **Mode collapse to interpolation:** the conditional diffusion may concentrate near the linear path between endpoints, reproducing marginal torsion distributions while lacking genuine oscillatory content. This is why the interpolation baseline and the *temporal* metrics (not just marginals) are the core test.\n2. **Endpoint memorization:** if performance collapses under start-frame-only conditioning, the model may be exploiting the endpoint rather than modeling dynamics.\n3. **Oversmoothing:** diffusion denoising can low-pass-filter fast oscillations even when amplitudes are approximately right, damping autocorrelation decay rates and spectral peaks.\n4. **Training/evaluation mismatch:** if training subsampling \u0394t differs from evaluation conditions, or if fine-step count N is chosen post hoc, apparent recovery may be an artifact; the multi-\u0394t sweep and pre-registration of N guard against this.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Controls (finalized set)\n\n| # | Control | Role |\n|---|---------|------|\n| (a) | **Linear/interpolation baseline** (linear, SLERP-based, or smooth-spline interpolation between the conditioning frames at the same fine step count) | Null model for H\u2080; the primary comparator. Any \"recovery\" MDGEN shows must exceed this. |\n| (b) | **Subsampled ground truth** (the coarse \u0394t-interval trajectory itself, resampled/interpolated to \u03b4t for metric computation) | Lower bound; demonstrates what subsampling destroys and validates metric sensitivity. |\n| (c) | **Fully sampled MD ground truth** | Upper bound / reference; the target the metrics must match. |\n| (d) | **Negative control:** time-shuffled and time-reversed versions of MDGEN's generated frames | Verifies the temporal metrics are genuinely sensitive to temporal ordering (they should fail dramatically; if they don't, the metric battery is invalid). |\n| (e) | **Paired comparison design:** every metric is computed per test segment (per protein \u00d7 per coarse segment), with MDGEN and the interpolation baseline evaluated on *identical* conditioning frames, compared via paired statistics | Removes segment-level variance (protein size, flexibility, basin count) from the comparison. |\n| (f) | **Start-frame-only conditioning variant \u2014 *optional ablation*, not a requirement** | Diagnoses the mechanism of any observed recovery (dynamics modeling vs. endpoint-guided interpolation). Omitted if compute-constrained. |\n\nAdditionally: the multi-\u0394t sweep (Section 4) acts as a dose\u2013response control \u2014 genuine recovery of fast dynamics should degrade monotonically with coarser conditioning; a flat response would suggest results are insensitive to the conditioning regime and hence suspect.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Analysis plan\n\n### 3.1 Primary metrics (torsion-angle temporal statistics)\n\nAll computed per dihedral (backbone \u03c6/\u03c8 and, where heavy-atom data permit, side-chain \u03c7\u2081) and per test segment, then aggregated via paired statistics:\n\n- **M1 \u2014 Torsion autocorrelation error:** C_k(\u03c4) = \u27e8\u0394\u03c6_k(t)\u0394\u03c6_k(t+\u03c4)\u27e9 (circular statistics for angles) for lags \u03c4 spanning the fine-timestep regime. Error metric: integrated absolute difference |C_gen(\u03c4) \u2212 C_true(\u03c4)| over the \u03c4 range, per dihedral, averaged per segment. *Note: because individual generated trajectories need not be sample-by-sample faithful, autocorrelations are compared at the ensemble level (statistics across generated replicates per conditioning frame), which is the statistically valid target.*\n- **M2 \u2014 Spectral distance:** power spectral density (Lomb\u2013Scargle or Welch on the regular \u03b4t grid) of each dihedral time series, restricted to frequencies in the fast regime (between 2/\u0394t and the Nyquist frequency of the native data). Distance: log-spectral distance or L1 on normalized PSDs, per dihedral per segment.\n- **M3 \u2014 Transition-time distribution:** for dihedrals with two-state basin structure (basins identified on ground truth only), the distribution of residence times and first-passage times between basins; compared via Wasserstein distance.\n- **M4 \u2014 Marginal torsion distributions** (reported but *not sufficient*): JS divergence of \u03c6/\u03c8 marginal and basin-population distributions. Included to show explicitly whether MDGEN matches marginals while failing M1\u2013M3 (failure mode 1).\n\n### 3.2 Secondary metrics (sanity checks, explicitly subordinate)\n\nRMSD/RMSF profiles, TIC-2 overlap, Ramachandran coverage, bond/angle geometry validity (physical plausibility of generated frames). These confirm the generated ensembles are globally sane and comparable to prior MDGEN-style evaluations. **They are reported as context only and do not enter the decision rule; a match on these alone does not constitute fast-dynamics recovery.**\n\n### 3.3 Statistical procedure and decision rule (pre-specified)\n\nFor each primary metric m \u2208 {M1, M2, M3}, compute the paired difference d_i = m_i(MDGEN) \u2212 m_i(interpolation) across test segments i (matched conditioning frames). Test H\u2080 via a paired permutation/sign-flip test (robust to the non-normal, bounded distributions of these error metrics) at **\u03b1 = 0.05**, with effect size reported as the median paired relative improvement and its bootstrap 95% CI (10,000 resamples).\n\n**Operational decision rule \u2014 \"recovery is real\" requires all of:**\n\n1. **Significance:** MDGEN significantly better than interpolation on M1 and M2 (the two core temporal metrics), one-sided, \u03b1 = 0.05, with family-wise control across M1\u2013M3 via Holm correction.\n2. **Effect size:** median paired relative reduction in error \u2265 a minimum threshold \u03b8 (initially set at \u03b8 = 20% relative error reduction for both M1 and M2; see calibration below).\n3. **Directional sanity:** MDGEN not worse than interpolation on M3 (no requirement of significance, given M3's lower statistical power), and M4 comparable or better.\n4. **Negative controls pass:** time-shuffled/reversed generated frames must show *substantially degraded* M1/M2 relative to unshuffled MDGEN (e.g., a large, individually verified gap); if shuffling does not degrade the metrics, the design declares the metric battery insufficient and no recovery claim is made.\n5. **Lower/upper bound sanity:** subsampled ground truth (b) must score clearly worse than full ground truth (c) on M1/M2, confirming the metrics detect the destruction subsampling causes.\n\n**Threshold calibration (planned step, not completed):** \u03b8 and the \"substantial gap\" in (4) cannot be set meaningfully a priori without dataset statistics. Calibration plan: on a small *validation-only* subset (never used for the primary test), compute the empirical distribution of (i) interpolation-vs-ground-truth errors (setting the scale of achievable improvement) and (ii) shuffled-vs-unshuffled metric gaps (setting metric noise floors). \u03b8 is then set at a fixed fraction (e.g., 25%) of the interpolation deficit that a perfect recovery would close, rounded to a value reportable across \u0394t conditions. The calibrated values are frozen before the primary comparison on the held-out test set.\n\n### 3.4 Reporting\n\nPer \u0394t condition: paired effect sizes with CIs for M1\u2013M3, M4 for context, secondary metrics as appendix tables, and an explicit statement of which decision-rule criteria passed/failed. Degradation curves of recovery quality vs. \u0394t reported for the sweep. No claim of recovery is made unless criterion (1)\u2013(5) are all met at the tested operating points.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Experimental configuration and feasibility\n\n### 4.1 Data and splits\n\n- **Dataset (assumed, per published MDGEN):** ATLAS-style all-atom protein MD trajectories with native fine timestep, and/or MDsim. If no fully sampled reference trajectories exist for a candidate dataset, **short native-timestep re-runs** of selected test systems (a few ns per protein at native \u03b4t, from equilibrated frames) serve as the fine-dynamics ground truth for evaluation \u2014 flagged as a possible required step.\n- **Splits:** protein-level train/val/test split (e.g., ~70/15/15 with diversity constraints on fold and length); within test proteins, coarse segments reserved for evaluation never appear in training. Both splits fixed and documented before generation.\n- **Subsampling grid:** \u0394t \u2208 {0.1, 1, 10} ns (or {0.05, 0.5, 5} ns for shorter trajectories), with a fixed fine step count N per coarse interval matched to the native \u03b4t so that generated \u03b4t matches ground-truth \u03b4t.\n\n### 4.2 Minimum viable configuration (MVC)\n\nThe smallest setup that still answers the question:\n- **Data:** \u2265 20 proteins total (\u2265 12 train, \u2265 8 test, spanning 3\u20134 fold classes); one \u0394t condition (1 ns) for the primary test, one additional \u0394t (10 ns) for the dose\u2013response check. Short native re-runs only for the 8 test proteins if no fully sampled data exist.\n- **Model:** the published MDGEN architecture at reduced scale, or the released checkpoint if one becomes available; N tuned only on validation.\n- **Statistics:** paired tests as above; with 8 test proteins \u00d7 \u2265 10 segments each (\u2265 80 paired observations), a one-sided paired test has adequate power to detect a 20% median error reduction given the within-segment pairing (exact power to be confirmed during calibration \u2014 flagged as an uncertainty).\n- **Baselines run:** interpolation (a), subsampled (b), shuffled negative control (d). Start-frame-only ablation (f) is the first thing dropped under constraint.\n\n### 4.3 Feasibility and compute \u2014 honest flags\n\n- **No compute is provisioned here.** This environment has no GPU, no scientific Python stack, no network. Original MDGEN training was GPU-intensive (multiple A100-class GPUs); feasibility depends entirely on resources the researcher provisions. Inference-only evaluation of an existing checkpoint is far cheaper and would be the MVC entry point if a checkpoint exists.\n- **Unknown intended \u0394t operating point:** the published setup (1 ns coarse / fine intermediates) is an assumption; the multi-\u0394t sweep is designed so the conclusion does not hinge on one choice, but the specific operating point the researcher cares about should be confirmed.\n- **Ground-truth availability risk:** if the target dataset is only available subsampled, native-timestep re-runs (Section 4.1) are needed; their force-field fidelity then bounds the interpretation (\"recovery of the reference force field's fast dynamics,\" not of physical reality).\n- **Replicate count for ensemble statistics:** M1/M2 require multiple generated replicates per conditioning frame (e.g., \u2265 16 samples per segment) for stable ensemble statistics; this multiplies inference cost and is included in the MVC budget.\n\n### 4.4 Summary of assumptions (distinct from fixed task requirements)\n\n**Fixed by the task:** design-only; no claimed results; question is recovery of fast torsion dynamics missing under subsampling.\n\n**Assumptions I am making (all revisable by researcher clarification):**\n1. MDGEN method details follow the published description (chromatic SE(3)-equivariant diffusion, endpoint-conditioned, trained on subsampled segments); no implementation is available to verify this.\n2. Fully sampled ground truth exists or can be cheaply re-run for test systems.\n3. Torsion-angle oscillation statistics at native \u03b4t are stationary enough over evaluation windows to be a valid reference.\n4. Datasets resembling ATLAS/MDsim are the intended substrate; the intended \u0394t operating point is near the published default.\n5. Compute (GPU) will be available for training or at minimum for inference from a checkpoint.\n6. Numeric decision thresholds (\u03b1, \u03b8, calibration procedure) are acceptable as pre-specified placeholders pending the planned calibration step on validation data.\n\n**Key uncertainties:** statistical power at small test-set sizes; whether ensemble-level autocorrelation agreement is achievable by any conditional generative model or only by trajectory-level fidelity (which would make even a \"good\" MDGEN fail M1/M2 as defined \u2014 in which case the calibration step should be revisited before declaring the hypothesis untestable); metric sensitivity to force-field-dependent fast librations that may not generalize across proteins.\n\n---\n\n*Deliverable ends. No experiment was executed; no results are claimed.*", "evidence": "Repository observations supporting this design: /workspace contains only task.json (464 bytes) with the question, context, and a track.md note \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" Environment checks: python3 present but numpy/mdtraj absent (ModuleNotFoundError), no GPU driver (nvidia-smi failure), network disabled. Therefore no repo-specific facts inform the design; MDGEN's architecture, conditioning scheme, subsampling regime, and original datasets are grounded in the published method (Schneuing et al., 2024, arXiv:2409.12174) and explicitly labeled as published-method knowledge rather than workspace-verified. The design incorporates all approved refinements: precise hypothesis with null; mechanistic rationale for why subsampling destroys fast torsion dynamics (aliasing below Nyquist, loss of short-time autocorrelation and transition timing); failure modes MDGEN could exhibit (mode collapse to interpolation, endpoint memorization, oversmoothing); the finalized control set (a)-(e) with the start-frame-only variant marked as an optional ablation; an operational pre-specified decision rule replacing the vague tolerance band (paired sign-flip/permutation test at alpha=0.05 with Holm correction across M1-M3, effect-size threshold theta with a stated calibration procedure on validation data, negative-control gating, and lower/upper bound sanity checks); secondary global metrics (RMSD/RMSF, TIC, Ramachandran) explicitly subordinate and excluded from the decision rule; honest feasibility flags (no GPU here, possible need for native-timestep re-runs as ground truth, unknown operating point); and a minimum viable configuration (8 test proteins, 2 Delta-t conditions, inference-only entry point if a checkpoint exists) so the design is feasible rather than aspirational. No experiment was run and no outcome is claimed.", "stage": "result", "summary": "Final self-contained design document delivered for MDGEN fast-dynamics recovery experiment", "uncertainties": ["Ground-truth availability: if only subsampled trajectories exist for candidate datasets, short native-timestep re-runs become a required data-generation step, and force-field fidelity then bounds interpretation.", "No verified MDGEN implementation, checkpoint, or dataset exists in the workspace; architecture and training details are from the published paper and may differ in the researcher's intended setup.", "Compute: original-scale MDGEN training is GPU-intensive and no compute is provisioned here; feasibility hinges on researcher-provisioned resources, with inference-from-checkpoint as the minimum viable entry point.", "Statistical power: the minimum viable configuration (>=80 paired segment-level observations) is estimated but not verified; exact power depends on within-segment metric variance learned during calibration.", "Metric validity risk: ensemble-level autocorrelation/spectral agreement may be unattainable for any conditional generative model if only trajectory-level fidelity suffices; the calibration step should be revisited before declaring the hypothesis untestable.", "Unknown intended Delta-t operating point and fine-step count N; the multi-Delta-t sweep mitigates but the researcher's target regime should be confirmed."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-fa234f233b304d639a08cb2c364c9e1a", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Final self-contained design document delivered for MDGEN fast-dynamics recovery experiment", "kind": "A